 "etapas": [
  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "What the data says and what the headline adds",
     "texto": [
      "A data point and a headline do not say the same thing, even when they use the same words. The data is what was measured; the headline is an interpretation.\n\nBefore taking something as certain, ask yourself three things:\n\n1. **Who was asked?** If the survey was voluntary and online, the people who answered were the ones who wanted to.\n2. **How many answered?** One hundred and twenty-eight out of two hundred and forty is not “most of the population”.\n3. **What can be concluded?** From a sample like that you can say “some”, not “almost all”.\n\nThe headline that jumps from the sample to the population makes a leap that the data does not authorise."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "The language of data",
     "texto": [
      "Data is presented with graded expressions. These are not the same:\n\n- **casi todos** (more than ninety per cent)\n- **la mayoría** (more than half)\n- **muchos** (a significant part, with no figure)\n- **algunos** (a minority)\n\nAnd there are verbs that mark the strength of the conclusion: **sugerir** is weak, **indicar** is medium, **demostrar** is strong. A headline usually goes up a level: what the report presents as a suggestion, the headline presents as proof.\n\nWatch out also for **representativo**: a sample is representative if it was chosen at random and reflects the group. If the people who chose to take part were the ones who picked it, it is not."
     ],
